' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' zip/doc.mfb — the package's compiler-validated documentation.
'
' These `DOC` blocks are what an importer sees. A compiled `.mfp` ships without
' source, so an ordinary `'` comment reaches nobody outside this directory: only
' a DOC block is carried in the package binary, checked against the declaration
' it names, and rendered by `mfb doc`.
'
' Placement is free and the header line is the only link to the target, so they
' live together here rather than being scattered through the implementation.
'
' Error codes are the `errorCode::` values, written as `mfb spec diagnostics
' error-codes` writes them: 7-705-0003 is errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat, and so on.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DOC
  PACKAGE
  DESC Read and write ZIP archives, including ZIP64.
  DESC
  DESC An archive is opened either from bytes already in memory or from a file
  DESC you have open, through the same `open` call, and both forms give you the
  DESC same entries with the same contents.
  DESC
  DESC Opening a file-backed archive reads only the list of entries, and reading
  DESC one entry holds only that entry, so an archive far larger than memory is
  DESC still readable one entry at a time. Writing to a file works the same way:
  DESC `openWriter` puts each entry in the file as it is added.
  INFO Every entry you read is checked against the CRC-32 the archive records for
  INFO it. An entry whose bytes do not match raises `zip::ErrorChecksum` rather
  INFO than returning data that fails its own checksum.
  SEC `extractTo` treats an archive as untrusted input. It checks every entry
  SEC before writing any byte, refuses to overwrite an existing file, and opens
  SEC each file with containment enforced, so a name that is absolute, contains
  SEC `..`, or resolves outside the target through a symlink already on disk is
  SEC refused. A failed extraction removes what it created.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Archive
  DESC An open archive: where its bytes are, what it contains, and its comment.
  DESC
  DESC An `Archive` over a file refers to the file you opened without taking it
  DESC over. The file stays open until you close it, and closing it makes later
  DESC reads through this archive fail with `errorCode::ErrResourceClosed`.
  PROP source Where the archive's bytes come from. Internal.
  PROP entries Every entry, in the order the archive lists them.
  PROP comment The archive-level comment, or the empty string.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Entry
  DESC One entry in an archive.
  DESC
  DESC Every field is read from the archive's index, so all of it is available
  DESC before any of the entry's contents are read.
  PROP name The entry's name, with `/` separating directories, as the archive spells it.
  PROP isDirectory Whether the name ends in `/`, which is how zip marks a directory.
  PROP method How the data is stored: 0 for stored, 8 for deflated.
  PROP size The entry's size once uncompressed.
  PROP compressedSize The number of bytes the entry occupies in the archive.
  PROP crc The CRC-32 the archive records for the entry's contents.
  PROP modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch. An entry written with 0 reads back as 315532800, the earliest time the DOS fields name.
  PROP mode The Unix `st_mode` with file-type and permission bits, or 0 when not written on a Unix-like host.
  PROP comment The entry's own comment, or the empty string.
  PROP headerOffset Where the entry's local header sits in the archive. Used by `read`.
  PROP flags The general-purpose flag word from the central directory.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Builder
  DESC An archive under construction, held in memory.
  DESC
  DESC Start one with `create`, add to it, and call `finish` when you are done.
  DESC Each `add` call gives you back a new builder; the one you passed in is
  DESC unchanged. For an archive too large to hold in memory, use `openWriter`.
  PROP pending The entries added so far. Internal.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Writer
  DESC An archive being written to a file, one entry at a time.
  DESC
  DESC Start one with `openWriter`, write entries with `writeFile`, `writeText` and
  DESC `writeDirectory`, and call `finishWriter` to complete the archive. Each write
  DESC gives you back the updated writer; use that one, since the one you passed in
  DESC now refers to the same file at an earlier position.
  PROP sink The file the archive is written to. Internal.
  PROP spool The central directory headers, held until `finishWriter`. Internal.
  PROP offset How many bytes have been written to the file. Internal.
  PROP count How many entries have been written. Internal.
END DOC

DOC INTERNAL
  TYPE Source
  DESC Where an archive's bytes come from: in memory, or in a file you have open. Use `open` instead.
  PROP fromFile Whether the archive is read from a file rather than from memory.
  PROP bytes The archive's bytes, when they are held in memory.
  PROP handle The file the archive is read from, when it is a file.
END DOC

DOC INTERNAL
  TYPE Pending
  DESC One entry a `Builder` or a `Writer` holds before it is laid out.
  PROP name The entry's name.
  PROP isDirectory Whether this entry is a directory.
  PROP method How the data is stored.
  PROP size The entry's uncompressed size.
  PROP compressedSize The bytes the entry's data occupies in the archive.
  PROP crc The CRC-32 of the entry's contents.
  PROP payload The bytes as they appear in the archive. Empty in a Writer.
  PROP modifiedSeconds The modification time, in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  PROP mode The Unix `st_mode` to record.
  PROP headerOffset Where the entry's local header is placed.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC open(List OF Byte)
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Open an archive held in memory.
  INFO The argument needs a typed binding: an untyped list literal such as
  INFO `zip::open([1, 2, 3])` does not say which of the two `open` forms you
  INFO meant, so it does not compile.
  ARG data The archive's bytes.
  RET The opened archive.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 The bytes are not a well-formed archive, or an entry's name is not decodable.
  ERROR 7-705-0007 The archive uses a feature this package does not support, such as multiple disks.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      MUT b AS zip::Builder = zip::create()
      b = zip::addText(b, "notes.txt", "hello")
      LET raw AS List OF Byte = zip::finish(b)
      LET archive AS zip::Archive = zip::open(raw)
      io::print(toString(len(zip::entries(archive))) & " entries")
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC open(fs.File)
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Open an archive from a file you have open.
  DESC
  DESC The whole file is never read. Opening reads only the list of entries, and
  DESC `read` afterwards holds one entry at a time, so an archive far larger than
  DESC memory is still readable one entry at a time.
  INFO The file stays yours. This package never closes it, and it is left at the
  INFO same read position it was found at.
  ARG file The file to read the archive from.
  RET The opened archive.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 The bytes are not a well-formed archive, or an entry's name is not decodable.
  ERROR 7-705-0007 The archive uses a feature this package does not support, such as multiple disks.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      RES f AS fs::File = fs::createTempFile()
      fs::writeAllBytes(f, zip::finish(zip::addText(zip::create(), "notes.txt", "hello")))
      fs::flush(f)
      LET archive AS zip::Archive = zip::open(f)
      FOR EACH entry IN zip::entries(archive)
        io::print(entry.name)
      NEXT
      fs::close(f)
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC entries
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Every entry in the archive, in the order the archive lists them.
  ARG archive The archive to list.
  RET The entries.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC comment
  GROUP Reading
  DESC The archive-level comment, or the empty string when there is none.
  ARG archive The archive to read the comment from.
  RET The comment.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC has
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Whether an entry with exactly this name is present.
  ARG archive The archive to search.
  ARG name The name to look for, matched exactly.
  RET TRUE when the name is present.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC find
  GROUP Reading
  DESC The entry with exactly this name.
  DESC
  DESC Zip permits an archive to hold the same name twice. This returns the first
  DESC such entry in the order the archive lists them, which is the one `unzip`
  DESC lists first. `extractTo` refuses such an archive outright, rather than
  DESC picking one of the two.
  ARG archive The archive to search.
  ARG name The name to look for, matched exactly.
  RET The matching entry.
  ERROR 7-705-0004 No entry has that name.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC read
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Read one entry's uncompressed bytes.
  DESC
  DESC The bytes are checked against the CRC-32 the archive records for them. A
  DESC directory entry reads as the empty list.
  INFO `maxBytes` is checked against the size the archive records before any data
  INFO is read, so an oversized entry costs nothing to refuse. That is what makes
  INFO it safe to open an archive you did not create and look through it.
  ARG archive The archive to read from.
  ARG entry The entry to read, as returned by `find` or `entries`.
  ARG maxBytes The largest uncompressed size to accept. Defaults to 64 MiB.
  RET The entry's bytes.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The entry is larger than `maxBytes`.
  ERROR 7-705-0007 The entry is encrypted, or uses a compression method other than stored or deflate.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 The entry contradicts the archive's index, or the archive is truncated.
  ERROR 9315-0001 The entry's bytes do not match its recorded CRC-32.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      LET raw AS List OF Byte = zip::finish(zip::addText(zip::create(), "notes.txt", "hello"))
      LET archive AS zip::Archive = zip::open(raw)
      LET bytes AS List OF Byte = zip::read(archive, zip::find(archive, "notes.txt"))
      io::print(toString(len(bytes)) & " bytes")
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC readText
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Read one entry and decode it as UTF-8 text.
  DESC
  DESC Everything `read` does, plus a strict decode: an entry that is not valid
  DESC UTF-8 is refused rather than returned with replacement characters.
  ARG archive The archive to read from.
  ARG entry The entry to read.
  ARG maxBytes The largest uncompressed size to accept. Defaults to 64 MiB.
  RET The entry's text.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 The entry is not valid UTF-8, or contradicts the archive's index.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The entry is larger than `maxBytes`.
  ERROR 9315-0001 The entry's bytes do not match its recorded CRC-32.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC create
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Start building a new archive in memory.
  DESC
  DESC For an archive too large to hold in memory, use `openWriter`.
  RET An empty builder.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      MUT b AS zip::Builder = zip::create()
      b = zip::addText(b, "notes.txt", "hello")
      io::print(toString(len(zip::finish(b))) & " bytes")
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addFile
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a file entry holding `data`.
  DESC
  DESC The entry is deflated when that makes it smaller and stored when it does
  DESC not, so an incompressible file is never made bigger by compressing it.
  INFO Refusing an unsafe name here uses the same rules `extractTo` applies. Two entries
  INFO that would extract to one path are not checked here; `extractTo` refuses them.
  ARG builder The builder to add to. It is not modified; the new builder is returned.
  ARG name The entry's name, with `/` separating directories.
  ARG data The entry's contents.
  ARG store Pass TRUE to store the data without compressing it. Defaults to FALSE.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch, from 0 to 4294967295. 0 writes the earliest DOS time, 1980-01-01.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits, from 0o0000 to 0o7777. 0 means 0o644.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 The name is unsafe: empty, absolute, containing `..`, a backslash or a NUL byte, naming a Windows drive, or with an empty or `.` component.
  ERROR 7-705-0002 The modification time or the mode cannot be recorded.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The name is longer than 65535 bytes.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addText
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a file entry holding the UTF-8 bytes of `text`.
  ARG builder The builder to add to.
  ARG name The entry's name.
  ARG text The entry's contents.
  ARG store Pass TRUE to store the text without compressing it. Defaults to FALSE.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch. Defaults to 0, which writes 1980-01-01.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 0, meaning 0o644.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 The name is unsafe.
  ERROR 7-705-0002 The modification time or the mode cannot be recorded.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The name is longer than 65535 bytes.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addDirectory
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a directory entry. A trailing `/` is added when the name lacks one.
  DESC
  DESC Directory entries are optional in a zip, since a file named `a/b.txt`
  DESC implies `a/`, but most tools list them.
  ARG builder The builder to add to.
  ARG name The directory's name.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch. Defaults to 0, which writes 1980-01-01.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 0, meaning 0o755.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 The name is unsafe.
  ERROR 7-705-0002 The modification time or the mode cannot be recorded.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC finish
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Finish the archive and return its bytes.
  DESC
  DESC The result is deterministic: the same calls in the same order always
  DESC produce the same bytes. ZIP64 records are written only when the archive
  DESC needs them, so an ordinary archive is an ordinary zip that any reader
  DESC accepts.
  WARN The whole archive is built in memory. For a large archive, use `openWriter`.
  ARG builder The builder to finish.
  ARG comment An archive-level comment. Defaults to none.
  RET The archive's bytes.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The comment is longer than 65535 bytes.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC openWriter
  GROUP Streaming
  DESC Start writing an archive to a file, which must be empty.
  DESC
  DESC Each entry's contents are written to the file as it is added, so the archive
  DESC never has to fit in memory. Only the central directory, about 50 bytes and the
  DESC name per entry, is held until `finishWriter`. The file stays yours: this package
  DESC never closes it, so close it after `finishWriter`.
  INFO Offsets in a zip are counted from the start of the file, so the file must be
  INFO empty. An archive written after existing bytes would point at the wrong places.
  ARG file An open file, empty, to write the archive to.
  RET A writer for the archive.
  ERROR 7-705-0002 The file is not empty.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT fs

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      RES f AS fs::File = fs::createTempFile()
      MUT w AS zip::Writer = zip::openWriter(f)
      w = zip::writeText(w, "notes.txt", "hello")
      zip::finishWriter(w)
      fs::close(f)
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC writeFile
  GROUP Streaming
  DESC Write a file entry holding `data` into the writer's file, now.
  DESC
  DESC The entry is deflated when that makes it smaller and stored when it does not.
  INFO Use the writer this returns, not the one passed in: see `openWriter`.
  ARG writer The writer to write to.
  ARG name The entry's name, with `/` separating directories.
  ARG data The entry's contents.
  ARG store Pass TRUE to store the data without compressing it. Defaults to FALSE.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch. Defaults to 0, which writes 1980-01-01.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 0, meaning 0o644.
  RET The updated writer.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 The name is unsafe. Nothing is written.
  ERROR 7-705-0002 The modification time or the mode cannot be recorded. Nothing is written.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC writeText
  GROUP Streaming
  DESC Write a file entry holding the UTF-8 bytes of `text` into the writer's file.
  ARG writer The writer to write to.
  ARG name The entry's name.
  ARG text The entry's contents.
  ARG store Pass TRUE to store the text without compressing it. Defaults to FALSE.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch. Defaults to 0, which writes 1980-01-01.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 0, meaning 0o644.
  RET The updated writer.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 The name is unsafe. Nothing is written.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC writeDirectory
  GROUP Streaming
  DESC Write a directory entry into the writer's file.
  ARG writer The writer to write to.
  ARG name The directory's name.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch. Defaults to 0, which writes 1980-01-01.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 0, meaning 0o755.
  RET The updated writer.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 The name is unsafe. Nothing is written.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC finishWriter
  GROUP Streaming
  DESC Write the central directory, completing the archive in the file.
  DESC
  DESC The file is flushed, so a write error surfaces here. Call this once, and do
  DESC not write to the writer afterwards.
  ARG writer The writer to finish.
  ARG comment An archive-level comment. Defaults to none.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The comment is longer than 65535 bytes.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT fs

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      RES f AS fs::File = fs::createTempFile()
      MUT w AS zip::Writer = zip::openWriter(f)
      w = zip::writeText(w, "a.txt", "one")
      w = zip::writeText(w, "b.txt", "two")
      zip::finishWriter(w, "two files")
      fs::close(f)
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC extractTo
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Extract every entry into `directory`, returning how many files were written.
  DESC
  DESC `directory` is created, with any missing parents, when it does not exist.
  DESC Directories in the archive are created but not counted.
  SEC Every entry is checked before any byte is written. A name is refused when it is
  SEC empty, absolute, contains `..`, a backslash or a NUL byte, or names a Windows
  SEC drive. Two entries that extract to one path, or a file where another entry needs
  SEC a directory, are refused. A path that already exists is refused: extraction never
  SEC overwrites a file. Each file is opened with containment enforced, so nothing is
  SEC written outside `directory`, even through a symlink already on disk.
  SEC A failure after writing has started removes every file and directory this call
  SEC created, then raises the original error.
  INFO File permissions and modification times are not restored, because `fs` cannot
  INFO set either. Extracted files are created user-only (0600, less the umask).
  ARG archive The archive to extract.
  ARG directory The directory to extract into. Created if it does not exist.
  ARG maxTotalBytes The largest total uncompressed size to write. Defaults to 1 GiB.
  RET The number of files written.
  ERROR 7-703-0002 An entry name is unsafe to write, or resolves outside `directory`.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 Two entries extract to one path, an entry contradicts its local header, or a file is needed where a directory is.
  ERROR 7-705-0005 A path that extraction would write to already exists, or the target is a file.
  ERROR 7-705-0007 An entry is encrypted, or uses an unsupported compression method.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The archive's total uncompressed size is over `maxTotalBytes`.
  ERROR 9315-0001 An entry's bytes do not match its recorded CRC-32. Nothing it created is left behind.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT zip
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    FUNC main() AS Nothing
      MUT b AS zip::Builder = zip::create()
      b = zip::addText(b, "notes/todo.txt", "buy milk")
      LET raw AS List OF Byte = zip::finish(b)
      LET archive AS zip::Archive = zip::open(raw)
      LET target AS String = fs::pathJoin([fs::tempDirectory(), "zip-doc-example"])
      io::print(toString(zip::extractTo(archive, target)) & " files")
      ' The target did not exist, so extractTo created it. Remove what it wrote, so
      ' the example can run again.
      fs::deleteFile(fs::pathJoin([target, "notes", "todo.txt"]))
      fs::deleteDirectory(fs::pathJoin([target, "notes"]))
      fs::deleteDirectory(target)
    END FUNC
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
